# snapback configuration — example
#
# Copy the section you need to the right place and edit it:
#
#   project-local:  <project>/.snapshotrc      (created by: snapback.sh init --local)
#   global:         ~/.snapshotrc              (created by: snapback.sh sampleconfig > ~/.snapshotrc)
#
# snapback.sh reads, in order of priority:
#   1. ./.snapshotrc in the current directory (project-local mode)
#   2. an explicit --config PATH, or $SNAPSHOTRC
#   3. ~/.snapshotrc (global mode)
#
# The file is plain shell assignments, sourced by the tool. Quote any value
# containing spaces: DIRS is a space-separated list, so unquoted paths with spaces
# break it.


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# PROJECT-LOCAL (recommended for repositories and agent workflows)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Snapshots live inside the project, so a rollback needs no global state and each
# project keeps its own history. Create it with:
#
#   cd /path/to/project
#   ./snapback.sh init --local                          # snapshots in ./.snapshots
#   ./snapback.sh init --local --snapshots-dir .snapback # or any directory you prefer
#
# init writes this file, creates the snapshot directory and adds both the snapshot
# directory and .snapshotrc to .gitignore.

# What to back up. '.' means the whole project.
DIRS='.'

# Where snapshots are stored. Relative, and it must stay inside the project. Use the
# same directory you passed to --snapshots-dir. It is excluded from its own
# snapshots, so pointing it elsewhere never makes snapshots of the snapshots.
TARGETBASE='./.snapback'

# Optional extra rsync flags. Keep them quoted.
#   --max-size=10m      skip files larger than 10MB
#   --exclude=*.pyc     skip Python bytecode
#   --exclude=build/    skip a build directory
RSYNC_PARAMS=''

# Retention (optional; these are the defaults):
# h_snaps=23     hourly snapshots kept
# d_snaps=7      daily snapshots kept
# w_snaps=4      weekly snapshots kept
# m_snaps=12     monthly snapshots kept


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# GLOBAL (snapshots outside the project, absolute paths required)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# DIRS='/home/you/Documents /home/you/Projects'
# TARGETBASE='/home/you/.Snapshots'
# RSYNC_PARAMS=''
#
# In global mode each DIRS entry is stored in the snapshot under its own directory
# name. Global mode does not apply .gitignore filtering.


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Restoring — read this before your first rollback
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ./snapback.sh recover <snapshot>     restore the snapshot. Never deletes anything.
#   --dry-run                          show what would change, change nothing
#   --keep-existing                    restore only what is missing; never overwrite
#   --full                             also delete files added since the snapshot
#
# ./snapback.sh undel <snapshot>       restore deleted files only; never deletes
#
# Paths that snapshots never contain are protected from deletion by any restore,
# including --full: .git, gitignored files and directories, the snapshot directory
# itself, and this config file.
#
# Verify a rollback before trusting it: after recovering, check that .git still
# exists and that your gitignored content is still there.
